.Dd June 5, 2026
.Dt MAC_CAPABILITY_COALITION 4
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm mac_capability_coalition
.Nd capability-based resource group management
.Sh SYNOPSIS
To load at boot:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
echo 'mac_capability_coalition_load="YES"' >> /boot/loader.conf
.Ed
.Pp
Wire protocol header:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
#include <dev/mac_capability/mac_capability_coalition_proto.h>
.Ed
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
service groups processes and other resources under a single capability handle
for collective operations.
A coalition can contain process descriptors, jail descriptors,
.Xr mac_capability 4
instances, sockets, shared memory objects, and nested coalitions.
.Pp
Operations are issued synchronously via
.Dv MAC_CAPABILITY_CALL
or asynchronously via
.Dv MAC_CAPABILITY_SENDMSG .
Asynchronous replies and state-change notifications are delivered as
.Dv MAC_CAPABILITY_RECVMSG
payloads.
.Xr kqueue 2
.Dv EVFILT_READ
reports notification readiness;
.Dv EVFILT_WRITE
reports send-queue readiness.
.Pp
Member file descriptors are passed as attached fds, not as integer fd numbers
in the payload.
.Pp
The default termination signal is
.Dv SIGKILL .
.Ss Operations
All operations begin with a common request header:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
struct coalition_req_hdr {
	uint32_t	op;
};
.Ed
.Pp
Most operations return a generic reply:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
struct coalition_reply {
	int32_t		status;	/* 0 = success, errno on failure */
};
.Ed
.Pp
The following operations are defined:
.Bl -tag -width "Dv COALITION_OP_SET_WATCHDOG"
.It Dv COALITION_OP_ENLIST
Attach a member file descriptor to the coalition.
The member fd is passed as an attached fd.
Accepted descriptor types are
.Dv DTYPE_PROCDESC ,
.Dv DTYPE_JAILDESC ,
.Dv DTYPE_MAC_CAPABILITY ,
.Dv DTYPE_SOCKET ,
and
.Dv DTYPE_SHM .
A
.Dv DTYPE_MAC_CAPABILITY
descriptor backed by the coalition service is treated as a nested coalition.
Returns
.Vt struct coalition_reply .
.It Dv COALITION_OP_JOIN
The calling process joins the coalition without requiring a file descriptor.
This operation is
.Dv MAC_CAPABILITY_CALL
only; it cannot be used with
.Dv MAC_CAPABILITY_SENDMSG
because the service taskqueue has no caller process context.
Returns
.Vt struct coalition_reply .
.It Dv COALITION_OP_ENLIST_SET
Attach multiple member file descriptors in a single call.
All member fds are passed as attached fds.
Stops on the first error.
Returns:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
struct coalition_enlist_set_reply {
	int32_t		status;
	uint32_t	enlisted;  /* count of successfully enlisted members */
};
.Ed
.It Dv COALITION_OP_TERMINATE
Terminate all coalition members.
Processes receive the configured signal
.Pq default Dv SIGKILL ,
jails are removed via
.Fn prison_remove ,
sockets are shut down, and
.Xr mac_capability 4
members are revoked.
Returns
.Vt struct coalition_reply .
.It Dv COALITION_OP_STAT
Query coalition membership and state.
Returns:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
struct coalition_stat_reply {
	int32_t		status;
	uint32_t	member_count;
	uint32_t	flags;          /* COF_* */
	int32_t		signal;
	uint32_t	nesting_depth;
	uint32_t	nested_count;   /* nested coalition members */
	uint32_t	mac_capability_count;   /* non-coalition DTYPE_MAC_CAPABILITY */
	uint32_t	process_count;
	uint32_t	jail_count;
	uint32_t	other_count;
	/* identity extension */
	uint64_t	id;             /* permanent coalition id */
	uint64_t	responsible_id; /* responsible parent id, 0 = unset */
	int32_t		leader_pid;     /* current leader, 0 = none */
	int32_t		responsible_leader_pid;
};
.Ed
.Pp
Every coalition carries a permanent 64-bit
.Fa id ,
assigned at creation and never reused while the kernel runs; 0 is never
issued.
The fields from
.Fa id
on were appended after the first release: a caller that offers only
.Dv COALITION_STAT_REPLY_V1_LEN
bytes receives the original layout, one that offers the full structure
receives it all.
A coalition responsible for itself reports its own id in
.Fa responsible_id .
.Fa responsible_leader_pid
is the responsible coalition's current leader, or 0 when it has none or is
gone.
.It Dv COALITION_OP_SET_RESPONSIBLE
Record the coalition's responsible parent: the coalition on whose behalf
this one exists.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
struct coalition_set_responsible_req {
	uint32_t	op;
	uint32_t	flags;   /* COALITION_RESP_* when no fd is attached */
};
.Ed
.Pp
The parent is named by the attached descriptor: a coalition instance names
that coalition, and a process descriptor names the coalition its process is a
member of.
The coalition service holds attached descriptors in the kernel and never
delivers them onward, so attaching is not a transfer: a descriptor confined
with
.Dv CAP_XFER_NONE
may be attached (to this and every other coalition operation), and a
.Dv CAP_XFER_ONCE
descriptor is not consumed.
With no descriptor,
.Dv COALITION_RESP_CALLER
names the calling process's own coalition and
.Dv COALITION_RESP_SELF
makes the target the root of its own chain (a shared provider that answers
for its own existence).
The edge is set once
.Pq Er EALREADY
and is immutable afterwards; a chain may not loop
.Pq Er ELOOP
and is bounded in length.
It is attribution only: membership, signals, nesting, and lifetime are not
affected, and the edge outlives the parent's termination so a process can
always be walked back, through
.Xr ps 1
.Pq Fl o Cm coal , rcoal , rpid
or
.Xr procstat 1
.Pq Cm coalition ,
to the session or system that caused it.
.It Dv COALITION_OP_SET_SIGNAL
Configure the signal used for group termination.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
struct coalition_set_signal_req {
	uint32_t	op;
	int32_t		signal;
};
.Ed
.Pp
A
.Fa signal
of 0 selects release: when the coalition terminates or is closed, its
process members are dropped from it but not signalled.
An attribution-only coalition, such as the one that stands for a login
session, uses this so that ending the record never kills a detached process
that outlived it.
Graceful and deadline escalation still deliver
.Dv SIGKILL
explicitly.
.Pp
Returns
.Vt struct coalition_reply .
.It Dv COALITION_OP_GRACEFUL
Perform graceful termination: deliver the specified signal to all process
members, wait for the timeout, then
.Dv SIGKILL
any survivors.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
struct coalition_graceful_req {
	uint32_t	op;
	int32_t		signal;
	uint32_t	timeout_ms;
};
.Ed
.Pp
Returns
.Vt struct coalition_reply .
.It Dv COALITION_OP_SET_DEADLINE
Set a time-bounded lifetime for the coalition.
When the deadline expires, the configured signal is delivered.
If
.Fa grace_ms
is nonzero, a grace period follows before
.Dv SIGKILL .
Setting
.Fa timeout_ms
to zero cancels the deadline.
Setting
.Fa signal
to zero causes immediate
.Dv SIGKILL
with no grace period.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
struct coalition_set_deadline_req {
	uint32_t	op;
	uint32_t	timeout_ms;
	int32_t		signal;     /* 0 = immediate SIGKILL */
	uint32_t	grace_ms;
};
.Ed
.Pp
Returns
.Vt struct coalition_reply .
.It Dv COALITION_OP_SET_WATCHDOG
Enable a dead-man switch that requires periodic heartbeats.
If no heartbeat arrives within
.Fa timeout_ms ,
the coalition is terminated.
Setting
.Fa timeout_ms
to zero disables the watchdog.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
struct coalition_set_watchdog_req {
	uint32_t	op;
	uint32_t	timeout_ms;
};
.Ed
.Pp
Returns
.Vt struct coalition_reply .
.It Dv COALITION_OP_HEARTBEAT
Reset the watchdog timer.
Request is a plain
.Vt struct coalition_req_hdr .
Returns
.Vt struct coalition_reply .
.It Dv COALITION_OP_SET_LEADER
Designate a leader process.
The leader's process descriptor is passed as an attached fd.
If the leader exits, the coalition is terminated.
Omitting the attached fd clears the leader.
Returns
.Vt struct coalition_reply .
.It Dv COALITION_OP_RUSAGE
Query aggregate resource usage across all process members.
Returns:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
struct coalition_rusage_reply {
	int32_t		status;
	uint32_t	nprocs;
	uint32_t	nthreads;
	uint32_t	_pad;
	uint64_t	rss_bytes;
	uint64_t	vsz_bytes;
	uint64_t	user_usec;
	uint64_t	sys_usec;
	uint64_t	inblock;
	uint64_t	oublock;
	uint64_t	majflt;
	uint64_t	minflt;
};
.Ed
.It Dv COALITION_OP_LEDGER
Return the coalition's resource counters, read from the container the kernel's
accounting framework maintains for it.
Unlike
.Dv COALITION_OP_RUSAGE
this never walks the member list, so it is safe to call often and from a
policy that must not block.
Nothing is sampled, so nothing goes stale and
.Va age_ms
is always zero; the field is kept because it is part of the reply.
.Dv COALITION_LEDGER_REFRESH
no longer refreshes anything and instead means: judge the declared ceilings
before replying, rather than waiting for the next sweep.
Returns
.Vt struct coalition_ledger_reply .
.It Dv COALITION_OP_SET_LIMIT
Declare ceilings on the coalition's own resident memory, address space and CPU,
or remove any of them with a figure of zero.
See
.Sx Memory Ceiling
and
.Sx CPU Ceiling .
Returns
.Vt struct coalition_reply .
.It Dv COALITION_OP_SET_IDLE_EXIT
Declare the coalition able to be put away when nothing is using it, or clear
that again.
See
.Sx Idle Exit .
Returns
.Vt struct coalition_reply .
.It Dv COALITION_OP_BAND
Read the coalition's bands, and with
.Dv COALITION_BAND_SET_FLOOR
set the floor first.
Returns
.Vt struct coalition_band_reply ,
describing the state after any change.
.It Dv COALITION_OP_ASSERT
Return a new descriptor that asserts a band.
Returns
.Vt struct coalition_band_reply
and one file descriptor.
See
.Sx Bands And Assertions .
.El
.Ss Bands And Assertions
A coalition has a band: how hard the system should try to keep it running when
memory runs short.
Bands are ordered, from
.Dv COALITION_BAND_IDLE
through
.Dv COALITION_BAND_BACKGROUND ,
.Dv COALITION_BAND_STANDARD ,
.Dv COALITION_BAND_INTERACTIVE ,
to
.Dv COALITION_BAND_CRITICAL .
.Pp
The band is not a single value somebody sets and must then remember to take
back.
It has two parts.
The
.Em floor
is the band the coalition always has, set by its launcher from the bundle
manifest.
An
.Em assertion
is a descriptor:
.Dv COALITION_OP_ASSERT
returns a new descriptor, and while that descriptor is open the coalition's
band is at least the asserted one.
When it is closed, deliberately or because the process holding it exited or
crashed, the assertion goes away with it.
.Pp
The effective band is the highest asserted band, or the floor if nothing is
asserted.
Because an assertion is an ordinary capability it can be transferred, so a
client may hand a provider the right to keep the client's own work alive while
a request is in flight, and it can be revoked like anything else.
Nothing has to reconcile a table of who asked for what, and no crash can leave
a coalition pinned at a high band.
.Pp
An assertion descriptor accepts only
.Dv COALITION_OP_BAND ,
which reports the band it asserts and the coalition's effective band.
It carries no other authority: it cannot enlist, signal, or read the membership
of the coalition it holds up, and the assertion service cannot be connected to
directly.
Computing the effective band takes no lock, so a memory policy can rank every
coalition from a context in which it can wait for nothing.
.Ss Memory Pressure
When the system runs short of memory every coalition is sampled and notified,
so a unit can drop caches of its own accord before anything else happens.
.Pp
The pass then
.Em hands down
the coalitions the system is least interested in keeping: the lowest bands
first and, within a band, the largest footprint, since that frees the most for
the least disruption.
Handing a coalition down advises its members' address spaces
.Dv MADV_DONTNEED ,
which clears page references and moves pages to the front of the reclaim queue
without discarding anything: dirty data is kept, the coalition keeps running,
and it faults back whatever it still needs.
What it has given up is its claim on that memory ahead of everyone else.
.Pp
Handing down terminates nothing.
When memory runs out anyway, the coalition is also the unit the system gives up
entirely: see
.Sx Out Of Memory .
.Pp
A coalition above
.Va pressure_band_ceiling
is never handed down, so the critical band, where CORE units sit, is left
alone.
The same coalition is not handed down again within
.Va pressure_interval_ms ,
and coalitions with a smaller footprint than
.Va pressure_min_kb
are ignored.
.Ss Member Types
The following descriptor types are accepted by
.Dv COALITION_OP_ENLIST :
.Bl -tag -width "Dv DTYPE_MAC_CAPABILITY (coalition)" -compact
.It Dv DTYPE_PROCDESC
Process descriptor.
Requires
.Dv CAP_PDKILL
rights under Capsicum.
.It Dv DTYPE_JAILDESC
Jail descriptor.
Requires
.Dv CAP_JAIL_REMOVE
rights.
.It Dv DTYPE_MAC_CAPABILITY
A
.Xr mac_capability 4
capability instance.
If the instance is backed by the coalition service, it is treated as a
nested coalition; otherwise it is revoked on termination.
.It Dv DTYPE_SOCKET
Socket.
Requires
.Dv CAP_SHUTDOWN
rights.
Terminated via
.Fn soshutdown SHUT_RDWR .
.It Dv DTYPE_SHM
Shared memory object.
Requires
.Dv CAP_FTRUNCATE
rights.
.El
.Pp
Nested coalitions are limited to a maximum depth of 16
.Pq Dv COALITION_MAX_NESTING .
Cycles are detected and rejected at enlist time.
.Ss Deadline
The
.Dv COALITION_OP_SET_DEADLINE
operation sets a time-bounded lifetime.
When the timer fires:
.Bl -enum -compact
.It
If
.Fa signal
is nonzero, that signal is delivered to all process members.
.It
If
.Fa grace_ms
is nonzero, a grace period starts.
.It
After the grace period (or immediately if
.Fa grace_ms
is zero or
.Fa signal
is zero), remaining members receive
.Dv SIGKILL .
.El
.Ss Watchdog
The
.Dv COALITION_OP_SET_WATCHDOG
operation enables a dead-man switch.
The holder must periodically send
.Dv COALITION_OP_HEARTBEAT
before the timeout expires.
If the watchdog fires, the coalition is terminated with the configured signal.
.Ss Notifications
Asynchronous state-change events are delivered as
.Dv MAC_CAPABILITY_RECVMSG
payloads:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
struct coalition_event_msg {
	uint32_t	flags;   /* COALITION_NOTE_* */
};
.Ed
.Bl -column "Dv COALITION_NOTE_MEMBER_REMOVED" "0x0080" -offset indent
.It Sy Constant Ta Sy Value
.It Dv COALITION_NOTE_MEMBER_ADDED Ta 0x0001
.It Dv COALITION_NOTE_MEMBER_REMOVED Ta 0x0002
.It Dv COALITION_NOTE_TERMINATING Ta 0x0004
.It Dv COALITION_NOTE_TERMINATED Ta 0x0008
.It Dv COALITION_NOTE_LEADER_DIED Ta 0x0010
.It Dv COALITION_NOTE_DEADLINE_FIRED Ta 0x0020
.It Dv COALITION_NOTE_WATCHDOG_FIRED Ta 0x0040
.It Dv COALITION_NOTE_GRACE_STARTED Ta 0x0080
.El
.Ss Coalition Flags
The
.Fa flags
field of
.Vt struct coalition_stat_reply
contains a bitmask of:
.Bl -column "Dv COF_LEADER_MONITOR" "0x0020" "Grace period active, new members rejected" -offset indent
.It Sy Constant Ta Sy Value Ta Sy Meaning
.It Dv COF_TERMINATING Ta 0x0001 Ta Coalition is being terminated
.It Dv COF_DEADLINE_ACTIVE Ta 0x0002 Ta Deadline timer is running
.It Dv COF_DEADLINE_GRACE Ta 0x0004 Ta Deadline grace period is active
.It Dv COF_WATCHDOG_ACTIVE Ta 0x0008 Ta Watchdog timer is running
.It Dv COF_HAS_LEADER Ta 0x0010 Ta A leader process is designated
.It Dv COF_LEADER_MONITOR Ta 0x0020 Ta Leader monitor holds a reference
.It Dv COF_GRACE_ACTIVE Ta 0x0040 Ta Grace period active, new members rejected
.It Dv COF_RESPONSIBLE Ta 0x0100 Ta Responsible parent has been set
.El
.Ss What A Coalition Declares, And What Decides
The coalition is the only subject.
Nothing is ever declared about a process: a process takes part only by being a
member, which is how its footprint is counted, or by holding an assertion,
which is a capability it was given rather than a property of being a process.
There is consequently nothing to reconcile between the members of a coalition.
.Pp
Three things are declared, by whoever holds the coalition, which in practice
means the launcher acting on a bundle's manifest: a band floor, how much the
system should want to keep this work; optionally a footprint ceiling, what it
may hold; and optionally idle-exit eligibility, whether it can be put away and
brought back.
.Pp
One thing is dynamic: assertions.
Holding one raises the band and means the work is in use.
Nothing else modulates any of it.
.Pp
Band and idle-exit eligibility answer different questions and are kept apart.
The band decides what to give up first when memory is short.
Eligibility decides whether something can be put away when nobody is using it.
An idle exit therefore does not consult the band, and a low band does not imply
that anything is idle.
.Ss Why A Coalition Died
Every coalition death has exactly one reason, reported identically in the event
its holder receives, in the log, and in the
.Fn coalition-kill
probe, so that
.Dq what happened to my unit
has one answer wherever it is asked.
.Pp
.Bl -tag -width "Dv COALITION_KILL_SYSTEM_MEMORY" -compact
.It Dv COALITION_KILL_NONE
the handle was closed; no policy was involved
.It Dv COALITION_KILL_REQUESTED
a holder asked
.It Dv COALITION_KILL_DEADLINE
it ran past its deadline
.It Dv COALITION_KILL_WATCHDOG
it stopped answering
.It Dv COALITION_KILL_LEADER
its leader went away
.It Dv COALITION_KILL_OVER_CEILING
it exceeded the figure declared for it
.It Dv COALITION_KILL_SYSTEM_MEMORY
the machine ran out and this was the least wanted
.It Dv COALITION_KILL_IDLE
it was idle, and able to come back
.It Dv COALITION_KILL_OVER_CPU
it exceeded the CPU ceiling declared for it
.El
.Pp
The policy reasons are counted separately, in
.Va limit_kills ,
.Va oom_kills ,
.Va idle_kills
and
.Va cpu_kills ,
because a unit over its own memory budget, the machine being short, work being
put away to be brought back, and a unit spinning are four different situations
and should not be read off one number.
.Ss Idle Exit
Some work can be put away and brought back without losing anything, such as an
on-demand unit that starts again the next time somebody asks for it.
Whether a coalition is that kind of work cannot be inferred, so it is declared
with
.Dv COALITION_OP_SET_IDLE_EXIT ,
and no coalition is eligible by default.
A unit that would be restarted immediately should not be declared, because
putting it away achieves nothing.
.Pp
Eligibility says the work
.Em can
be put away.
Assertions say whether it should be right now: a coalition with any live
assertion is in use and is left alone, and taking an assertion restarts the
idle clock.
An eligible coalition with no assertions must also have been that way for its
declared age before it is put away, so work that goes quiet for a moment is not
taken from under its user.
.Ss The Periodic Sweep
A ceiling and an idle timer would mean nothing if they were only examined when
the system ran short, so a sweep runs every
.Va sweep_interval_ms .
It acts on any coalition that is over a ceiling and puts away any that has been
idle long enough.
.Pp
It deliberately does not notify and does not hand anything down; those belong to
memory pressure, which is a different question.
This is about promises a coalition made about itself being kept whether or not
the machine is busy.
.Pp
The sweep walks no member lists.
Every figure it judges is a counter the accounting framework already maintains,
so a pass costs the same whatever the coalitions on the machine are made of,
and the interval decides only how promptly a breach is noticed, not how stale
the figures are.
.Ss Memory Ceiling
A coalition may be given a ceiling on its footprint.
This is not a per-process limit, which a unit escapes by forking: it is a figure
for the unit and every helper it starts, taken together.
.Pp
A coalition with no ceiling is never limited, so nothing happens until
something declares one.
Two figures may be declared.
.Va vmem_bytes
is a ceiling on address space, which the framework charges at the mapping path,
so the figure is exact: it needs no sampling and is never behind.
.Va memory_bytes
is a ceiling on resident memory, which the framework refreshes from the page
daemon's pass, so that figure is at most one of those passes old and is zero
for a process too young to have been looked at.
.Pp
Exact describes the figure, not how soon a breach is acted on.
No ceiling is enforced at the moment a resource is taken; charging at the
mapping path is what keeps the count honest, and a ceiling is judged when
something looks: the periodic sweep, a memory-pressure pass, or a request
carrying
.Dv COALITION_LEDGER_REFRESH .
Putting the judgement on the charge path would mean a coalition lookup on every
mapping the system makes, which is a price the whole machine would pay for a
figure very few units declare.
.Pp
A coalition that goes over and comes back between two looks is never penalised,
which is the right behaviour for a figure describing a whole unit's working
set.
.Pp
Because every ceiling is judged from the container, a kernel booted without
resource accounting
.Pq Va kern.racct.enable Ns =0
has nothing to judge, and
.Dv COALITION_OP_SET_LIMIT
answers
.Er EOPNOTSUPP
rather than accepting a figure nothing would act on.
.Pp
A breach always notifies, through
.Dv COALITION_NOTE_LIMIT .
With
.Dv COALITION_LIMIT_KILL
it also terminates the coalition, and it does so regardless of band: a
coalition that has exceeded a figure declared for it is over budget whether or
not the system happens to be short, and the band only decides what to give up
first among coalitions that are within theirs.
A coalition that comes back under its ceiling is rearmed, so a unit that drifts
over and recovers is told once per excursion rather than once per look.
.Ss CPU Ceiling
A coalition may also be given a CPU ceiling, declared with the same
.Dv COALITION_OP_SET_LIMIT
as a percentage of one processor: 100 is one processor's worth, 400 is four,
and zero is no ceiling.
.Pp
It is a rate, not a total.
A total only ever grows, so a unit that has been running for a week breaches
any figure worth setting.
What is judged is
.Dv RACCT_PCTCPU
on the coalition's container: the decaying average the accounting framework
already keeps beside every other figure, clamped there to the number of
processors the machine has.
Nothing in this driver measures it.
.Pp
It covers the whole unit, including CPU spent by members that have since left.
That falls out of the framework rather than being arranged here: CPU is neither
reclaimable nor decaying, so it is never taken back out of a container when a
member leaves.
A coalition therefore cannot stay under its ceiling by doing its work in
short-lived children.
.Pp
A breach notifies through
.Dv COALITION_NOTE_LIMIT
and, with
.Dv COALITION_LIMIT_KILL ,
terminates the coalition with
.Dv COALITION_KILL_OVER_CPU .
The CPU ceiling shares the
.Dv COALITION_LIMIT_KILL
flag and the rearm behaviour with the memory ceilings, so a unit that goes over
and settles is told once per excursion.
.Pp
The rate is refreshed once per sweep and read everywhere else, so it has one
owner and one cadence.
A coalition that is not swept therefore has no CPU ceiling in effect, which is
the same thing as saying the sweep is what enforces it.
.Pp
Enlisting a process that has already spent CPU does not read as a burst.
Joining moves the whole of a process's accounting into the container, which for
a total is right and for a rate is a step that never happened, so the rate's
starting point is moved with it.
.Pp
This has no equivalent in Darwin, whose CPU limits are per-task and report
through an exception port rather than describing a unit.
.Ss Jails
A coalition may hold a jail, which governs that jail's lifetime: the jail is
removed when the coalition goes.
It also accounts for what runs inside it.
.Pp
Only a process carries a pointer to a container, so a jail's processes are
charged individually even though none of them is enlisted.
A process is charged when it attaches to the jail, when it is forked inside
one, and, for work already running, when the jail is enlisted.
They do not become members: a jail's processes are accounted, not enlisted, so
the member count, the member limit and what a termination signals are all
unchanged.
.Pp
A process enlisted in its own right is charged there and nothing overrides it.
An individual enlistment is the more specific statement about whose work a
process is, and a jail the process merely happens to be in does not displace
it.
Where jails are nested and more than one is held, the innermost wins, for the
same reason.
.Pp
Nothing is undone when a process leaves a jail or a jail stops being held.
A container is reference counted, so a pointer into one is always safe, and
when a process exits it gives back everything reclaimable and drops its
reference on its own.
.Ss Out Of Memory
When the system is about to run out of memory, something has to be given up.
The stock choice is the single largest process, which is very often the most
important process on the machine precisely because it is the biggest.
.Pp
This driver offers a different basis for the decision: the lowest band first,
and within a band the largest footprint, because that frees the most for the
least.
The unit given up is the whole coalition, so a unit and its helpers go together
rather than leaving a decapitated remainder behind.
Candidates are ranked on the footprint their accounting container carries,
which is the only figure that counts everything a coalition is holding: a
coalition may hold a jail, and a jail's processes are charged to it without
being members of it.
Walking the member list instead would count process members only, so a unit
doing its work inside a jail it held would look empty and never be chosen.
Reading a counter also takes no lock that can block, which matters here more
than anywhere: this runs in the page daemon, and a page daemon that waits is
waiting on itself.
.Pp
A coalition above
.Va oom_band_ceiling
is never chosen, which is what keeps CORE units out of reach, and an assertion
is enough to lift a coalition above it for as long as the assertion is held.
If no coalition is eligible the decision is declined and the stock
largest-process choice runs unchanged, so this can only change which process
dies, never prevent the system from recovering.
.Pp
The choice is offered after the existing out-of-memory rate limit, so it is
throttled exactly as the stock choice is.
Every kill is logged with the coalition, the party responsible for it, the band
that made it expendable, the footprint that made it the choice, and the number
of members, and fires the
.Fn oom-kill
probe with the same.
.Ss Sysctl Tunables
The out-of-memory knobs are
.Va oom_kill ,
.Va oom_band_ceiling
and the read-only counter
.Va oom_kills .
Terminations for a coalition's own ceiling are counted separately in
.Va limit_kills ,
and for its CPU ceiling in
.Va cpu_kills ,
since exceeding a declared figure and being given up because the machine ran
out are different events.
The idle-exit knobs are
.Va idle_exit
and
.Va idle_min_age_ms ,
with
.Va idle_kills
counting what has been put away; the sweep is controlled by
.Va sweep
and
.Va sweep_interval_ms .
The memory-pressure knobs are
.Va pressure_reclaim
(hand coalitions down at all),
.Va pressure_band_ceiling ,
.Va pressure_max_targets ,
.Va pressure_interval_ms ,
.Va pressure_min_kb ,
and the read-only counter
.Va pressure_reclaims ,
all under
.Va kern.mac_capability_coalition .
.Bl -tag -width "Va kern.mac_capability_coalition.max_members"
.It Va kern.mac_capability_coalition.count
Number of active coalitions (read-only).
.It Va kern.mac_capability_coalition.max
Maximum number of coalitions; 0 means unlimited.
Soft limit, best-effort enforcement.
.It Va kern.mac_capability_coalition.max_members
Maximum total members across all coalitions; 0 means unlimited.
Soft limit, best-effort enforcement.
.It Va kern.mac_capability_coalition.members
Total members across all coalitions (read-only).
.El
.Sh OBSERVING COALITIONS
Two scripts ship in
.Pa /usr/share/dtrace
and are installed by default:
.Pa mac_capability-coalitions
traces this service in depth (every operation, timer, membership change and
teardown, with per-event counts on exit), and
.Pa mac_capability-responsibility
follows the whole story across both halves of the plane, pairing the
launcher's decisions (which party a unit answers to, a login session
getting its coalition, a session's units being withdrawn, a launch
constraint refusing an activation) with the kernel's outcomes (coalitions
created and closed, members signalled or released, memory-pressure passes).
.Pp
For the same relation at rest, without tracing:
.Xr ps 1
.Fl o Cm coal , rcoal , rpid ,
.Xr procstat 1
.Cm coalition ,
and
.Nm switchboardctl Cm tree .
.Sh DTRACE PROBES
The following DTrace probes are available under the
.Nm mac_capability_coalition
provider:
.Bl -tag -width "deadline-expire(phase, signal)"
.It Fn create coalition_id badge
Fired when a coalition is created.
.Ar badge
is the instance badge.
.It Fn enlist dtype error
Fired after an enlist attempt.
.Ar dtype
is the descriptor type;
.Ar error
is 0 on success or an errno.
.It Fn join pid error
Fired when a process joins.
.Ar pid
is the caller's PID;
.Ar error
is 0 on success.
.It Fn terminate member_count error
Fired on termination.
.Ar member_count
is the number of members at the time of termination.
.It Fn close member_count
Fired when the coalition handle is closed.
.It Fn member-exit pid
Fired when a member process exits.
.It Fn leader-exit pid
Fired when the designated leader process exits.
.It Fn band-floor coalition_id band effective
Fired when a launcher sets a coalition's band floor.
.It Fn band-assert coalition_id band effective pid
Fired when an assertion descriptor is minted.
.Ar pid
is the process that asked for it, which is the answer to
.Dq who is holding this coalition up .
.It Fn band-release coalition_id band effective pid
Fired when an assertion goes away, whether it was closed, revoked, or died
with its holder.
An assertion count that only rises is a leak; the
.Pa /usr/share/dtrace/mac_capability-bands
script names the holder.
.It Fn racct-join coalition_id pid
Fired when a process is charged to a coalition's accounting container, which
is what makes its resources count towards that coalition's ceilings.
.It Fn racct-leave coalition_id pid cpu_usec
Fired when a process leaves that container.
Everything reclaimable goes back; the CPU reported here stays with the
coalition, because the accounting framework never takes a non-reclaimable
resource back out.
.It Fn racct-jail coalition_id pid
Fired when a process is charged to a coalition because of the jail it is in,
rather than because it was enlisted.
Such a process is accounted for, not enlisted: it never becomes a member.
.It Fn racct-drain coalition_id cpu_usec rss_bytes
Fired when a coalition gives up its container, reporting what it still held.
CPU is expected there and resident memory is not: a non-zero figure means a
member left without giving it back.
.It Fn coalition-kill coalition_id responsible_id reason rss_bytes band
Fired once for every coalition death, whatever caused it, carrying the
.Dv COALITION_KILL_*
reason.
This is the probe to trace when something disappeared and the question is why.
.It Fn limit-breach coalition_id responsible_id rss_bytes limit_bytes killed
Fired when a coalition goes over the ceiling declared for it.
.Ar killed
is 1 if it is being terminated for it and 0 if it is only being told.
This is the probe for
.Dq which unit is over budget ,
as against
.Fn oom-kill ,
which answers
.Dq what did the system give up because it ran out .
.It Fn oom-kill coalition_id responsible_id band rss_bytes nprocs
Fired when a coalition is terminated to reclaim its memory.
This is the report to reach for after processes disappear.
.It Fn oom-decline ncoalitions
Fired when no coalition was eligible, so the stock largest-process choice ran.
.It Fn pressure-reclaim coalition_id band rss_bytes nprocs
Fired when a coalition is handed down: its pages were advised reclaimable
because memory is short and its band is one the system gives up first.
Nothing was terminated.
.It Fn member-spared coalition_id pid
Fired when a member is deliberately not signalled during a teardown because it
is the process doing the closing: a unit that tears down its own coalition does
not kill itself.
Without this a teardown trace shows every member but the survivor and gives no
reason for it, and which process that is depends on which holder closed the
coalition last.
.It Fn member-kill coalition_id responsible_id pid signal
Fired for each process member as a coalition tears down, before the signal
is sent.
A
.Ar signal
of 0 means the member is released rather than killed (see
.Dv COALITION_OP_SET_SIGNAL ) ,
so this probe reports both outcomes.
.Ar responsible_id
names the coalition on whose behalf this one existed, which is what makes
"why did my process die, and for whom" answerable from the probe alone.
.It Fn rehome pid from_coalition_id to_coalition_id
Fired when a membership that was only inherited is moved to another
coalition by a holder of the process descriptor.
.Ar to_coalition_id
is 0: the destination is reported by the
.Fn enlist
that follows.
.It Fn pressure coalitions notified
Fired once per low-memory pass, after every live coalition has been told.
.It Fn pressure-notify coalition_id members error
Fired per coalition during that pass;
.Ar error
is
.Er ESHUTDOWN
for a coalition that was terminating or closing and was skipped.
.It Fn fork-inherit parent_pid child_pid
Fired when a child process inherits coalition membership via
.Xr fork 2 .
.It Fn signal-set signal 0
Fired when the termination signal is changed.
.It Fn deadline-set timeout_ms signal grace_ms 0
Fired when a deadline is configured.
.It Fn deadline-expire phase signal
Fired when a deadline or grace period expires.
.Ar phase
is a string describing the expiry stage.
.It Fn watchdog-set timeout_ms 0
Fired when the watchdog is configured.
.It Fn watchdog-expire signal
Fired when the watchdog expires.
.It Fn heartbeat timeout_ms
Fired when a heartbeat resets the watchdog.
.Ar timeout_ms
is the remaining timeout.
.It Fn graceful signal timeout_ms 0
Fired when graceful termination is initiated.
.It Fn responsible-set id parent_id error
Fired after every
.Dv COALITION_OP_SET_RESPONSIBLE ;
.Ar parent_id
is 0 when the parent could not be resolved.
.It Fn call-done op error time
Fired after every operation completes.
.Ar op
is the operation code;
.Ar error
is the result;
.Ar time
is the elapsed time as an
.Vt sbintime_t .
.It Fn deny reason errno pid
Fired when an operation is denied.
.Ar reason
is a string description.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Create a coalition, enlist two processes, set a deadline, and terminate:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
/* Obtain coalition fd from capsule. */
int co_fd = obtain_coalition_fd();

/* Enlist a process descriptor. */
struct coalition_req_hdr req = { .op = COALITION_OP_ENLIST };
struct coalition_reply reply;
mac_capability_call(co_fd, &req, sizeof(req), &reply, sizeof(reply),
    &proc_fd, 1);

/* Set a 30-second deadline with SIGTERM + 5s grace. */
struct coalition_set_deadline_req dreq = {
	.op = COALITION_OP_SET_DEADLINE,
	.timeout_ms = 30000,
	.signal = SIGTERM,
	.grace_ms = 5000,
};
mac_capability_call(co_fd, &dreq, sizeof(dreq), &reply, sizeof(reply),
    NULL, 0);

/* Enable watchdog with 10-second timeout. */
struct coalition_set_watchdog_req wreq = {
	.op = COALITION_OP_SET_WATCHDOG,
	.timeout_ms = 10000,
};
mac_capability_call(co_fd, &wreq, sizeof(wreq), &reply, sizeof(reply),
    NULL, 0);

/* Send heartbeats periodically. */
struct coalition_req_hdr hb = { .op = COALITION_OP_HEARTBEAT };
mac_capability_call(co_fd, &hb, sizeof(hb), &reply, sizeof(reply),
    NULL, 0);

/* Graceful shutdown: SIGTERM + 5s grace. */
struct coalition_graceful_req greq = {
	.op = COALITION_OP_GRACEFUL,
	.signal = SIGTERM,
	.timeout_ms = 5000,
};
mac_capability_call(co_fd, &greq, sizeof(greq), &reply, sizeof(reply),
    NULL, 0);
.Ed
.Sh ERRORS
Operations return raw
.Xr errno 2
values in the reply
.Fa status
field:
.Bl -tag -width Er
.It Bq Er EINVAL
Invalid operation code, signal number, or timeout value.
.It Bq Er ENOTCAPABLE
The member fd lacks the required Capsicum rights.
.It Bq Er ESRCH
The target process or member was not found.
.It Bq Er EPERM
Permission denied.
.It Bq Er ELOOP
Enlisting a nested coalition would exceed
.Dv COALITION_MAX_NESTING
or create a cycle.
.It Bq Er ENOMEM
Insufficient memory for a new member.
.It Bq Er EBUSY
The coalition is in a terminating or grace state and rejects new members.
.It Bq Er EALREADY
The responsible parent has already been set.
.It Bq Er EBADF
The descriptor attached to
.Dv COALITION_OP_SET_RESPONSIBLE
is neither a coalition nor a process descriptor, or names a coalition that
is gone.
.El
